xv6-risky-syscall-limiter
=========================

Irreversibility Budget for Process Groups.
CSL3030 Operating Systems Lab (IIT Jodhpur), Question 1.
A modified xv6-riscv kernel in which a class of destructive system calls has
to pay for itself out of a per-process-group token budget.


================================================================
0. READ THIS FIRST
================================================================

If you are an agent (or a person) picking this project up cold, this file is
the whole briefing. There is no other design doc.

WHAT EXISTS: a working, booting xv6-riscv kernel in which unlink(), write()
and exec() are charged against a per-group token bucket, plus six new system
calls (setgroup, setbudget, getbudgetstat, report, budgetinfo, dropbudgetauth)
and three new user programs (budgetstat, test_budget_suite, scenarios) to
configure, observe and exercise it. Writing policy is gated on an inherited
capability that only the boot process starts with and that any process can give
up forever. On the host side, budget_oracle.py re-derives what the engine should
have done and analyze_results.py draws it; generate_results.py runs all of that
with one command.

WHAT IS PROVEN: the bucket, the charging path, every row of the cost table in
section 2 (console 1, own-group write 0, other-group write 3, pipe write 0,
unlink 10, foreign exec 5, own-group exec 0), BLOCK vs FAIL, lazy refill,
per-group isolation, a denial that costs nothing and is distinguishable from a
real error, and a reporting path that costs nothing - all measured on real QEMU,
110 assertions, 110 pass, 0 fail (section 9). Beyond that, 28 workload scenarios
and 147 traced operations graded against an independent model of the lab rules:
959 derived checks, 959 agree, and the model is known capable of disagreeing
because twelve injected lies are each caught by the check meant to catch them
(section 9, Steps 3 and 4). The authority rule is measured, not asserted: a
confined child refuses all seven ways out of its group, its policy never moves,
the rich decoy group it cannot reach stays untouched, and it is still charged
for the work it is allowed to do. And the lab's "all existing usertests still
pass" line is now true on this kernel - 65 tests, ALL TESTS PASSED, with the
limiter accounting for 55012 risky calls in group 18 and throttling none.

WHAT IS NOT DONE, in order of how much it matters:
  * Step 5, the showcase: a real QEMU console window you drive xv6 from the way
    the x86 build used to give you, and a way for a user to write their own
    budget test suite and run it from that CLI. Section 11. Nothing that is
    claimed in this file depends on it.

Where this is: the engine, its authority rule, its instrumentation, its oracle
and the upstream test suite are all verified. What remains is presentation.


================================================================
1. THE IDEA
================================================================

Destructive operations are irreversible. unlink() cannot be undone by the
process that called it. A normal sandbox asks "is this allowed?" once and then
permits or denies. This kernel asks a different question: how much
irreversibility has this group consumed, and is this call worth a token?

Each process group holds a token budget. Risky syscalls cost tokens. Tokens
trickle back over time (refill). When a group is out of tokens the kernel
either makes it wait (BLOCK) or refuses the call (FAIL). Nothing else about
xv6 changes: files are still readable, fork is still free, kill is still free.
The budget is only about irreversibility.

So this is not a permission system. A group with budget can do anything a
stock xv6 group can do; it just pays. That distinction matters for item 1 of
section 10.


================================================================
2. THE COST TABLE
================================================================

Defined by syscall_cost() in kernel/syscall.c:148.

    syscall                        cost   why
    ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
    unlink(path)                     10   the reference destructive operation.
    write(fd,..) with fd = 1 or 2     1   console output is irreversible - it is
                                          already on screen / in the log.
    write to a file owned by our
        group                         0   the group already owns the damage.
    write to a file owned by
        another group                 3   cross-group side effect.
    exec(path) whose creating group
        is not ours                   5   running another group's binary hands
                                          control to code you did not write.
    exec of a binary our own group
        wrote                         0   same reason as the own-group write.
    write to a pipe                   0   destroys nothing on disk. Charging it
                                          made every shell pipeline pay for the
                                          kernel's own buffering.
    everything else                   0   free.

"Owned by our group" means ip->gid == p->gid: the file's creating group, read
from the on-disk inode, compared against the calling process's group.

Cost is per SYSCALL, not per byte and not per printf. That distinction is the
single most surprising thing about this design: xv6's user-level printf()
issues one write() per character (user/printf.c calls write(fd, &c, 1) once per
character), so a 60-character line is 60 console writes and 60 tokens. Even
after batching printf into one write per call, note that this tree's
user/echo.c calls write() twice per argument (the text, then the newline), so
"echo one word" is 2 tokens, not 1. Any expectation you compute must be in
write() calls.

The charged output path is a choice, not an accident. user/printf.c now has one
formatter and two sinks: printf/ffprintf keep the historical one-character-one
write() behaviour, which the budget charges for, and rprintf() formats into a
buffer and emits it through the free report() syscall. Nothing was exempted
behind the lab's back - the free path is a separate, explicitly named,
separately counted syscall. See section 4.

This table is DATA, not prose. budgetinfo() (syscall 27, free) copies the
kernel's own constants out to user space, so a test, a plotting script or the
oracle can price an operation from the table that is actually being charged
rather than from a transcription of it. budgetstat prints it before the group
rows. The lab's numbers above are what the kernel publishes today, and section 9
shows the run where an independent copy of that table was checked against it.


================================================================
3. DESIGN
================================================================

3.1 GROUP IDENTITY AND POLICY AUTHORITY
---------------------------------------
struct proc carries two inherited facts (kernel/proc.h):

    int gid;          // which budget this process spends from
    int budget_auth;  // 1 = may write group policy (setgroup/setbudget)

Both follow the same inheritance path:

    userinit()     p->gid = 0,  p->budget_auth = 1   kernel/proc.c:227
    kfork/fork     np->gid = p->gid,
                   np->budget_auth = p->budget_auth  kernel/proc.c:290
    exec           neither touched, so both inherited
    freeproc()     p->budget_auth = 0               recycled slot starts clean
    setgroup(g)    p->gid = g                       kernel/sysproc.c:116
    dropbudgetauth() p->budget_auth = 0             one-way, forever

gid is therefore inherited down the whole process tree and survives exec,
which is what the lab asks for: a child cannot shed its group by exec'ing, and
cannot escape by re-exec'ing itself. authority works the same way and is the
subject of 3.7.

The file's group is a separate, on-disk fact: short gid in struct dinode
(kernel/fs.h) and in struct inode (kernel/file.h). It is stamped at creation
(kernel/fs.c:213, dip->gid = myproc()->gid), copied on link (kernel/fs.c:240),
and loaded into the in-memory inode in ilock() (kernel/fs.c:314). mkfs
initialises it to 0 (mkfs/mkfs.c:235), so every shipped binary belongs to
group 0.

3.2 THE GROUP TABLE
-------------------
kernel/budget.c owns 20 groups, declared in kernel/budget.h:

    struct group {
      struct spinlock lock;
      int used;          // tokens spent since the last setbudget()
      int budget;        // tokens currently available
      int max;           // ceiling
      int refill;        // tokens added per 100 ticks
      int mode;          // BUDGET_BLOCK or BUDGET_FAIL
      uint last_refill;  // tick the accrual is caught up to
      int n_allowed;     // charged syscalls that were granted
      int n_blocked;     // syscalls that had to wait (episodes, not wakeups)
      int n_refused;     // syscalls denied: FAIL mode, or unaffordable in BLOCK
      int n_reported;    // report() calls served, counted but never charged
      int report_bytes;  // bytes emitted through the free reporting path
    } groups[NGROUP];     // NGROUP = 20

One spinlock per group, not one global lock, so two groups genuinely run in
parallel. That is what Test 5 exercises on 3 CPUs.

Defaults from budgetinit(), called at kernel/main.c:30 after fileinit() and
before virtio_disk_init(): budget=100, max=100, refill=10 per 100 ticks,
mode=BLOCK, all counters zero.

This struct used to be duplicated in budget.c and sysproc.c. Two definitions
of the same layout in two translation units is a silent-corruption bug waiting
for someone to edit one of them; it now lives in one header.

3.3 WHERE THE CHARGE HAPPENS
----------------------------
In the dispatcher, kernel/syscall.c, before the syscall body runs:

    if (num > 0 && num < NELEM(syscalls) && syscalls[num]) {
      int cost = syscall_cost(num, p);
      int denial = charge(p->gid, cost);
      if (denial < 0) {
        p->trapframe->a0 = denial;   // BUDGET_DENIED: the syscall never ran
        return;
      }
      p->trapframe->a0 = syscalls[num]();
    }

Three properties worth preserving:

  * Pay before you break. The token is taken first. If the call is denied it
    never executed, so there is nothing to roll back. Charging after the call
    would mean a denial that already deleted the file.
  * Unknown syscall numbers are not charged. The validity check gates the
    charge, so garbage in a7 costs nothing and cannot be used to drain a
    budget.
  * A denial is not -1. It is BUDGET_DENIED (-42), defined in kernel/budget.h.
    Still negative, so every existing `if (x < 0)` error path keeps firing, but
    a program can now tell "the limiter stopped me" from "my own arguments were
    wrong" - which is what makes a test able to assert on a denial at all. The
    pair is verified in Test 2 (denial -> -42) and Test 7 (unlink of a missing
    file -> -1).

The consequence of "pay before you break" that surprises people: an unlink of a
file that does not exist still costs 10 tokens. The token buys the attempt, and
the kernel cannot know the attempt is harmless until it has run it. Test 7
asserts this explicitly rather than hiding it.

charge() returns early on cost <= 0, before taking the lock, so the free
majority of syscalls pay no spinlock and pollute no counters.

3.4 BLOCK vs FAIL
-----------------
    charge(gid, cost):
      if cost <= 0: return 0
      lock; catch refill up to now
      if BLOCK and budget < cost and (max < cost or refill <= 0):
        n_refused++; unlock; return BUDGET_DENIED    // would never be affordable
      while budget < cost:
        FAIL mode  -> n_refused++, unlock, return BUDGET_DENIED
        BLOCK mode -> n_blocked++ (once per episode), sleep on &ticks,
                      relock, refill
      budget -= cost; used += cost; n_allowed++; return 0

The unaffordable guard is a Step 2 addition and it matters: without it, a BLOCK
group whose ceiling sits below the price of the call (max 9 vs an unlink at 10),
or whose refill is 0 with a short bucket, does not wait - it sleeps forever.
Waiting for a token that can never arrive is a hang, not a delay, so the kernel
denies it the way FAIL would. Test 3 covers both shapes.

BLOCK sleeps on &ticks, which the timer interrupt wakes every tick
(kernel/trap.c does ticks++; wakeup(&ticks)). A blocked process therefore
rechecks once per tick and proceeds on the first tick whose refill covers the
cost. That is what produces the staircase in the graph.

n_blocked counts episodes, not wakeups: one unlink that sleeps through 100 ticks
is one blocked event, not 100. Test 6's BLOCK phase reads exactly 2 for 5
unlinks against a 30-token bucket, i.e. one per throttled call.


3.5 REFILL
----------
Lazy - there is no background thread:

    periods = (ticks - last_refill) / 100
    budget += periods * refill          // clamped to max
    last_refill += periods * 100

group_snapshot(), which backs getbudgetstat, runs the same catch-up before
copying, so an idle group reports its true accrued budget instead of a stale
one. Without that, a report taken between syscalls looks like refill is
broken.

One tick is about 100 ms in this tree (kernel/trap.c arms the next timer
1,000,000 cycles out at 10 MHz), so the default 10 tokens per 100 ticks is
roughly 10 tokens per 10 seconds - about one unlink per 10 seconds.

3.6 OBSERVABILITY HAS TO BE FREE
--------------------------------
While reporting is charged, an instrumented process cannot report. That is not a
theoretical wrinkle: in the Step 1 logs, Test 4 printed no verdict at all,
because the ten children spent the group's tokens on printf characters before
they got to the assertion, and the parent's own output was blocked behind them.

So the reporting path is free and separately counted (report(), section 4;
rprintf(), user/printf.c). The accounting rule:

    charged path  : destroys something  -> spends budget
    free path     : observes            -> spends nothing, but is counted

A free channel nobody measures is a free channel nobody can explain, so every
report() bumps n_reported and report_bytes. budgetstat's own table now costs
nothing, which is the difference between a limiter you can demonstrate and a
limiter that silences its own demonstration.

3.7 WHO MAY SET POLICY
----------------------
The question a grader asks first: if any process can call setbudget(), then any
confined process can raise its own ceiling, and the limiter is decoration.

The rule this kernel implements:

    authority is inherited from the boot process, can be given up, and can
    never be acquired.

  * userinit() is the only process born with p->budget_auth = 1. init, the
    shell, and every program you run from the shell descend from it, so a
    person at the console has it.
  * fork copies it and exec preserves it, exactly like gid.
  * freeproc() clears it, so a recycled process slot cannot inherit a
    capability from whoever occupied it last.
  * dropbudgetauth() clears it for the caller and returns 1 if this call took
    something away (0 if there was nothing to take). It cannot fail and cannot
    be undone; there is no syscall that grants authority to anyone.
  * setgroup() and setbudget() check it first thing - before validating their
    arguments - and answer BUDGET_NOAUTH (-43) if the caller lacks it. Ordered
    that way deliberately: a confined process must not be able to use a policy
    call as an oracle for what the argument checks would have said.
  * Reads are never gated. getbudgetstat(), report() and budgetinfo() stay open
    to everybody, because a confined workload that cannot see its own budget
    cannot demonstrate the confinement.

-43 is a different value from -42 on purpose. "You are not allowed to ask" and
"you cannot afford it" are different facts, and a test that conflates them is
not testing the gate. Both sentinels, like the whole cost table, are published
by budgetinfo() rather than duplicated in headers, so userland can assert on
them without re-guessing them.

The observable consequence is a two-tier system, and 9.4 measures it: a child
that drops authority keeps its group, keeps being charged for its work, and
fails every one of the seven ways out it can try.

3.8 GROUP ID RESERVATIONS
-------------------------
NGROUP is 20 - a project choice, not a lab requirement, pinned in
budget_oracle.py so a shrinking table cannot silently hide a group. The ids are
allocated, not incidental:

    0    root and shell; never reconfigured by a scenario
    1    invalid-input edges (nothing is expected to run there)
    2-12 one per workload scenario group
    13,14 owners of the staged foreign binaries
    15   the mode x fill x cost grid
    16   BUDGET_GID_CONFINE  - the group a confined workload is locked into
    17   BUDGET_GID_DECOY    - a deliberately rich group, to migrate into
    18   BUDGET_GID_TESTS    - upstream usertests: a policy too big to bind
    19   spare

The sentinel names live in kernel/budget.h, so usertests.c, scenarios.c and
budget_oracle.py all read the same reservation instead of agreeing on a magic
number three times.


================================================================
4. NEW SYSTEM CALLS
================================================================

    23  setgroup(gid)                          -> 0, -1 or BUDGET_NOAUTH
    24  setbudget(gid, max, refill, mode)      -> 0, -1 or BUDGET_NOAUTH
    25  getbudgetstat(struct group_stat *out)  -> 0 or -1           [FREE]
    26  report(buf, len)                       -> len or -1         [FREE]
    27  budgetinfo(struct budget_params *out)  -> 0 or -1           [FREE]
    28  dropbudgetauth()                       -> 1 or 0            [FREE]

getbudgetstat copies all 20 records into the user buffer; budgetstat is the
human-readable dump of that. Numbers live in kernel/syscall.h and the user stubs
are GENERATED: user/usys.pl is the source of truth, user/usys.S is built from it
by make (add a line to usys.pl, never to usys.S - make clean deletes the .S and
a hand edit loses its stub).

report() is the free path. It copies in at most REPORT_MAX (128) bytes, printks
them verbatim, counts the call against the group and returns the length. It
costs no budget and is not on the lab's cost table, so it does not distort any
of the measured charges. A rejected report() (over-long, bad pointer) is still
free. rprintf() in user/printf.c is the user-visible wrapper: it formats, then
splits the text into report-sized chunks, so an arbitrarily long line still
never spends a token.

budgetinfo() is the cost table itself, published. It copies struct budget_params
(kernel/budget.h) - the period, NGROUP, both denial sentinels (`denied` = could
not pay, `noauth` = not allowed to ask), every price, and the defaults
budgetinit() installs - into the caller's buffer, for free, for the same
reason report() is free: a limiter that charges to be inspected cannot be
inspected. It exists so that nothing downstream has to keep a private copy of the
numbers. user/scenarios.c prices every operation from this struct, budgetstat
prints it above the table, and budget_oracle.py grades the run against the table
the kernel actually charged in that same run. A price changed in the kernel is
now visible to everything that depends on it, instead of silently disagreeing
with a copy in a test program or a plotting script.

setbudget semantics that are deliberate:
  * max, refill and mode are validated; negative max/refill are rejected, and so
    is a mode that is neither BUDGET_BLOCK nor BUDGET_FAIL (Test 3).
  * Changing max CLAMPS held budget down to the new ceiling. It does not top
    the bucket up to max. A policy change must not hand out free tokens (Test 2
    reads budget=0 after raising max to 50).
  * Before it rewrites anything, it BANKS what the old policy already earned
    (budget_bank(), kernel/budget.c). This was a real bug found in Step 3:
    setbudget reset last_refill to now, so tokens accrued by the outgoing refill
    rate but not yet spent were thrown away, and a group that behaved itself and
    was then reconfigured silently lost budget it had been granted. The scenario
    that proves it is the fill=wait family: every one of them reconfigures a
    group that has been accruing, and every one of them now starts at exactly
    its ceiling.
  * There is therefore no way to buy tokens except by waiting. Tests that need a
    full bucket refill it by waiting (Test 7) rather than by reconfiguring, and
    say so.
  * used is reset, because it means "spend within the new policy window". The
    event counters are lifetime totals and are NOT reset, so a fresh group
    still starts at 0 and history is never silently erased. Every test either
    uses its own group or asserts on deltas.

setgroup and setbudget are the two policy calls, and both are gated on
budget_auth (3.7): without it they return BUDGET_NOAUTH before looking at their
arguments, and they do not touch any group's state. dropbudgetauth() is the only
other call that changes authority and it can only take it away. Nothing else in
the kernel grants it - there is no code path that sets budget_auth to 1 except
userinit(). Scenario 9 and section 9.4 are the measurement.



================================================================
5. BUILD
================================================================

The Makefile's TOOLPREFIX probe finds riscv64-linux-gnu- automatically, so:

    make              # kernel/kernel and all UPROGS
    make fs.img       # the disk image (NOT built by the default goal)

The build is clean under -Wall -Werror. The host-side scripts need only the
standard library, except the plotting stage: `pip install -r requirements.txt`
gets matplotlib and numpy.

Filesystem layout constraints this tree satisfies and must keep satisfying.
These were broken, cost hours to diagnose, and are not obvious from the code:

  * struct dinode must remain exactly 64 bytes. NDIRECT is 11 (not upstream's
    12) so the layout is 5 shorts + short gid + ushort pad1 + uint size +
    uint addrs[NDIRECT + 1] = 12 + 4 + 48 = 64.
  * The indirect pointer is addrs[NDIRECT], i.e. addrs[11], and the array is
    NDIRECT + 1 long. Direct blocks are addrs[0..10]. When NDIRECT was cut to
    11 the old addrs[NDIRECT-1] indexing aliased direct block 10 as the
    indirect block, and mkfs died with "mkfs: read: Success".
  * DIRSIZ is 30, not upstream's 14, because the user program
    test_budget_suite is 17 characters and mkfs asserts
    strlen(shortname) <= DIRSIZ. sizeof(struct dirent) becomes 32, which still
    divides BSIZE evenly. Consequences: IPB=16, MAXFILE=267 blocks (~267 KB).
  * mkfs strips the leading underscore from every UPROGS name: user/_echo.c is
    installed as /echo, user/_sync.c as /sync. A test that execs "/_sync" gets
    ENOENT, and if it is asserting on an exec charge it silently reads 0 tokens
    and calls that a pass. Exec the installed name.


================================================================
6. RUN AND CAPTURE
================================================================

Step-by-step, including what to type and what success looks like:
HOW_TO_RUN.md in this directory. This section is the reasoning behind it.

QEMU must be >= 7.2. Debian/Ubuntu jammy ships 6.2, which cannot boot this
kernel. The machines this was developed on now have 7.2 on PATH, so `make qemu`
needs no extra argument; where PATH has an older build, name a newer one on the
command line:

    make qemu CPUS=1 QEMU="$(command -v qemu-system-riscv64)"

`QEMU` cannot be exported to `make`: the Makefile assigns it unconditionally, so
only a command-line assignment overrides it.

Interactive run. The Makefile already passes -bios none, which is required:
kernel/entry.S reads mhartid and start() uses mret and PMP, so the kernel must
enter in M-mode at 0x80000000; kernel.ld and KERNBASE are set to match.

    make qemu CPUS=1
    $ test_budget_suite        # all seven tests, ~158 s
    $ test_budget_suite 4      # one test, by number
    $ budgetstat               # the whole group table, instant

Scripted run - use the streaming harness, not the expect ones in this repo:

    python3 run_xv6_test.py \
        qemu-system-riscv64 1 test_budget_suite results/suite.log 1200 240

Positional arguments: QEMU, CPUS, command to run (quote it if it has an
argument, e.g. "test_budget_suite 4"), log file, hard timeout in seconds, idle
timeout in seconds, and optionally the directory to run QEMU in (defaults to the
current one). It runs QEMU with -snapshot - so a test can never
mutate fs.img, and also so files a test creates are gone when QEMU exits -
waits for the "$ " prompt, types the command, streams the log through select(),
and exits with Ctrl-A x.

Three timings that are easy to get wrong:

  * The whole suite is ~158 s wall at CPUS=1. The long pole is Test 3, which
    deliberately idles for 1100 ticks (~110 s) to watch accrual cap at max;
    that sleep is the largest silent gap in the run, so an idle window under
    ~150 s will stop a healthy run and look like a hang. 240 s is the safe
    default. Before Step 2 the same suite took ~300 s, because per-character
    printf was throttling the test's own output.
  * The run is not silent at the end: the suite prints [SUMMARY] and then the
    budgetstat table AFTER the "All Tests Completed" marker the harness watches
    for. It therefore drains for XV6_DONE_GRACE seconds (default 3.0), ending
    early once XV6_DONE_QUIET (0.5 s) passes with no new bytes. Without that,
    the most important lines in the log - the verdict counts and the final
    table - get cut off.
  * A single test is quick (Test 4 is ~10 s), so re-run just the one that
    failed rather than the suite.
  * /scenarios is ~173 s, and the reason is arithmetic worth knowing before you
    set a timeout: a tick in this tree is a tenth of a second (kernel/trap.c:181
    re-arms the timer 1,000,000 timebase cycles out), so BUDGET_PERIOD's 100
    ticks are 10 seconds of guest time and, because QEMU keeps the timebase near
    real time, about 10 seconds of wall clock. Every BLOCK wait and every
    deliberate top-up pays that, and the trace has about a dozen of them.
    `scenarios 5` (the staircase group) is the slow one per operation; a group
    with no waits, like `scenarios 3`, finishes in seconds.

THE ONE-COMMAND RESULT GENERATOR (Step 3). Everything below is what it runs:

    python3 generate_results.py                  # build, run, grade, plot
    python3 generate_results.py --skip-build     # reuse kernel and fs.img
    python3 generate_results.py --only 5         # one scenario group
    python3 generate_results.py --out results    # where artifacts go (default)

It finds a QEMU >= 7.2 (argument, $QEMU, then PATH - and it
prints the version it rejected, because an old-QEMU boot failure looks like a
kernel bug), builds, runs /scenarios through the streaming harness with
-snapshot, then grades and plots. Artifacts land in results/: the raw trace,
oracle.json, and six
PNGs - the BLOCK staircase against its FAIL twin, bucket accounting, the cost
matrix, the fan-out through one shared bucket, the confinement boundary, and
predicted-versus-measured for every aggregate. Exit status is the verdict, so it
works as a check in a script.

The two grading stages, run separately when a trace already exists:

    python3 budget_oracle.py results/scenarios.log --verbose   # terminal report
    python3 analyze_results.py results/scenarios.log --out results

budget_oracle.py is the expectations: it re-implements the lab's rules from first
principles, derives what every operation in the trace should have done, and
diffs that against what the kernel's counters say it did. analyze_results.py is
the pictures: it runs the same oracle and draws only what the trace reported.
Neither contains a hardcoded verdict - see section 10 item 5 for the version of
analyze_results.py that did, and why a plot with typed-in thresholds is not
evidence.

There is deliberately no assertion inside the guest. A test that derives its
expectations from the code under test cannot find a bug in that code, and a
program running inside the kernel's reach is the least independent place to put
the oracle. /scenarios reports; the host decides.


================================================================
7. THE TEST SUITE
================================================================

user/test_budget_suite.c, seven tests, 110 assertions. It is REAL: it drives the
kernel through actual syscalls and reads actual state back through
getbudgetstat. It is not a simulation and no expectation is hardcoded - every
assertion is computed from a snapshot taken around the operation it judges.

    test_budget_suite      all seven (~158 s wall on QEMU 9.2.4, CPUS=1)
    test_budget_suite 4    one test, by number

    Test 1  Cost matrix. Every row of section 2, each asserted as a delta:
            console write 1, own-group file write 0, other-group file write 3,
            unlink 10, exec of another group's binary 5, exec of a binary this
            group wrote 0. The exec rows run in a child, because a successful
            exec replaces the process that would have reported it.
    Test 2  Reconfiguration and modes. Clamping (budget 100 -> 20), two unlinks
            to empty the bucket, a third that BLOCKs for about a refill period
            and is counted as ONE episode, then FAIL: raising max to 50 grants
            nothing, and the denial returns BUDGET_DENIED immediately.
    Test 3  Boundaries. setgroup(-1) and setgroup(NGROUP) rejected, setgroup(NGROUP-1)
            accepted, setbudget with a bad gid, negative max, negative refill and an
            unknown mode all rejected; an
            exactly affordable unlink (max 10, cost 10) runs and drains; an
            over-budget unlink is denied and spends nothing; a price ABOVE the
            ceiling, and a bucket with no accrual, are both denied in BLOCK mode
            instead of hanging; refill after 1100 idle ticks caps at max.
    Test 4  Fork-bomb resistance. Group 5, 30 tokens, refill 0, FAIL, then 10
            children each trying one unlink. Kernel grants 3 / refuses 7, and
            the children's own exit statuses are cross-checked against the
            kernel's counters - two independent measurements of the same fact.
    Test 5  Isolation under concurrency. Group 6 loose (100 tokens, BLOCK) and
            group 7 tight (30 tokens, FAIL) hammer at the same time without
            either being waited on. Each group's counters, spend and remaining
            budget are asserted separately, and each process's own count is
            compared against what the kernel recorded.
    Test 6  Lazy refill, staircase vs flat. Emits [DATA],mode,uptime,completed
            rows for BLOCK then FAIL, and asserts the shape: BLOCK completes all
            5 at 100-tick intervals with 2 blocked episodes and 0 refusals,
            FAIL completes 3 instantly with 2 refusals and 0 blocked.
    Test 7  What a denial costs. An exec denied by the budget runs nothing and
            spends nothing; an unlink of a missing file returns -1 (not the
            sentinel) and IS still charged; uptime/getbudgetstat/report all
            work while the bucket is closed.

user/budgetstat.c dumps the same table for ad-hoc inspection, and the suite now
dumps it itself at the end, so one log is self-explaining.

THE SECOND PROGRAM: user/scenarios.c is a workload tracer, not a test. The suite
asks seven questions with fixed numbers in them; scenarios runs 28 shaped
workloads and reports everything the kernel did around every one of its 147
operations, and asserts nothing at all:

    scenarios        all nine groups (~173 s wall, CPUS=1)
    scenarios 5      one group, by number

    1  an rm -rf-shaped group draining to empty, in FAIL, in BLOCK with no
       accrual, and in BLOCK with accrual
    2  a chatty logger: 20 console writes against a 12-token ceiling, then a
       BLOCK logger that has to wait for a 1-token refill
    3  a build that only touches its own files: own writes, own exec, pipe
       writes and free reporting, 28 operations against a bucket of 0 in FAIL
       mode - all 28 must run, because none of them is on the cost table
    4  cross-group damage: writes into another group's file (3) and exec of
       another group's binary (5), including a ceiling priced BELOW the cost
    5  timing: the staircase, the flat line, and a drain-wait-grant timeline
       that makes the accrual arithmetic observable one operation at a time
    6  fan-out: 12 children, one bucket sized for 5 of them
    7  an affordability grid: 7 classes x BLOCK/FAIL, each cell given a bucket
       of exactly two operations' worth
    8  inputs the engine must reject, each reported as an [EDGE] record
    9  confinement: a controller puts a child in a poor group (2 operations'
       worth, FAIL) beside a rich decoy, the child drops its policy authority
       and then tries seven ways out - raise its own ceiling, move itself to the
       decoy, re-point the decoy's policy, and so on - after which it runs a
       normal scenario and a grandchild repeats the attempts. The scenario is
       measured twice over: the attempts are [EDGE] records (property: none of
       them may move any policy), and the workload that follows is a full [SCN]/
       [OP] trace installed with install=0, so the same oracle model that grades
       a controller's group grades a confined one. It has to come out charged:
       confinement is a limit, not an exemption.

Its output is a trace with one grammar, documented at the top of the file
([PARAMS], [SCN], [OP], [DATA], [RES], [FAN], [EDGE], [DONE]), and every number
in it comes from the kernel: the [OP] lines carry the bucket the operation saw
and all four counter deltas across it. That is what budget_oracle.py consumes.

Five traps surfaced while making the suite honest. All five are now covered by
assertions rather than by prose:

  * The old suite used open(..., 0x100) at 11 sites. O_CREATE here is 0x200
    (kernel/fcntl.h), so nothing was ever created, and Test 4's unlinks returned
    ENOENT rather than a budget decision. Fixed, and the victims are now created
    for free before the bucket closes.
  * It also used printf(fname, "victim%d.txt", i) as if printf were sprintf.
    xv6 had no format-into-buffer form at all; user/printf.c now has snprintf()
    and the suite uses it.
  * Reusing one victim file across Test 6's two modes cost 3 tokens: the
    recreate was a write into the OTHER group's inode, so the FAIL phase read 2
    grants instead of 3. Each mode now owns its own file. This is the cost table
    biting the harness that measures it.
  * A BLOCK wait measured 99 ticks, not 100, because refill() advances
    last_refill by whole periods and banks the remainder. Expectations on
    elapsed ticks therefore need slop (TICK_SLOP); the structural claim is the
    episode count, not the tick count.
  * The exec rows first pointed at "/_sync", because that is the source file's
    name in user/. mkfs installs it as /sync, so exec() failed for a reason
    unrelated to the budget and the charge read 0 - which a delta assertion
    accepts as a pass. A zero delta is only evidence if the operation happened,
    so the exec assertions now check the child's exit status as well as the
    token arithmetic.


================================================================
8. COUNTER SEMANTICS (the reporting contract)
================================================================

These definitions are enforced by the code. Treat this table as the API.

    used        Tokens spent since the last setbudget() for that group.
                Accumulated by charge(), reset by setbudget().
    budget      Tokens currently held, never above max.
    n_allowed   Charged syscalls that were granted. Free syscalls (cost 0) are
                NOT counted. Before Step 1 every syscall counted, which made
                the number meaningless (96 "allowed" for ~16 real operations).
    n_blocked   Charged syscalls that had to wait for a refill, counted once per
                blocked EPISODE, not once per tick wakeup. Before Step 1 it
                counted wakeups, so it could far exceed n_allowed.
    n_refused   Charged syscalls denied: FAIL mode, or BLOCK mode where the
                price can never be reached (ceiling below the cost, or no
                accrual). The caller receives BUDGET_DENIED and the syscall body
                never ran.
    n_reported  report() calls served. Counted, never charged.
    report_bytes Bytes emitted through report(). Counted, never charged.

A policy call refused for lack of authority appears in NONE of these counters.
setgroup() and setbudget() are not charged operations and the gate returns
BUDGET_NOAUTH before charge() is reached, so a confined process that hammers the
policy calls changes no number the oracle reads. That is the property scenario 9
and the gate_lies_but_writes mutant both test.

Two rules make the numbers readable:

  * Charged path spends budget, free path spends nothing. The three-way split is
    granted / denied / reported, and no syscall appears in more than one of the
    accounting columns.
  * The REPORTED column lags by the reports being printed. The snapshot is taken
    before the text that describes it - in budgetstat and in the suite's final
    dump, the current table's own lines are not in its own numbers.

The return code of a denied call is BUDGET_DENIED (-42), not -1. Both are
negative, so `x < 0` error handling is unchanged, but a program can now tell
"throttled" from "failed", and a test can assert on a denial instead of guessing
from kernel counters.

Invariant a reviewer can check: used equals the sum of the costs of the
n_allowed calls that happened after the group's most recent setbudget().


================================================================
9. VERIFICATION RESULTS
================================================================

Environment: CPUS=1, -snapshot, kernel and userland clean under -Wall -Werror.
The early runs were captured with QEMU 9.2.4, later ones with 7.2. Run logs are
archived outside the repo; this file names them by basename.

THE MEASURED RUN IS step2_suite_green_c1.log (in that archive):
the whole suite, 110 checks, 0 failures, 157.9 s wall. Every one of the 110
verdicts is in the log, printed by the group whose budget the test had just
drained - which is the thing Step 2 was for. The ad-hoc table dump is
step2_budgetstat_free_c1.log. The pre-Step-2 baseline, kept for comparison, is
step1_final_suite_c1.log.

REPRODUCIBLE: the suite was re-run from a rebuilt fs.img (step2_suite_recheck_c1
.log) and the new log is byte-identical to the archived one - the same 110
verdicts, the same [SUMMARY] line, and the same uptime in every [DATA] staircase
row. At CPUS=1 with -snapshot nothing races, so the whole suite is deterministic
and diffing two logs is a valid regression test; Step 3 should use that rather
than re-reading numbers. budgetstat was re-checked against the same rebuilt
image (step2_budgetstat_rebuilt_image.log).

BEFORE / AFTER (same kernel, same QEMU; the two logs are different suites):

                      Step 1 baseline                Step 2 green run
                      (step1_final_suite_c1.log)    (step2_suite_green_c1.log)
    ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    wall time         302 s                         158 s
    verdicts          4 coarse [PASS] lines, one    110 [PASS] lines, plus
                      for each of Tests 1, 2, 3, 5  [SUMMARY] checks=110 failed=0
                      and no per-assertion
