STEP 3 AND 4, THE WORKLOADS AND THE ORACLE - measured in results/scenarios.log:
28 scenarios, 147 traced operations, 959 derived checks, 0 failures, 173 s wall
at CPUS=1. Regenerate the whole thing with `python3 generate_results.py`.

The oracle is a second implementation of the lab's rules, written from the spec
and not from kernel/budget.c. It reads the trace, works out what each operation
should have done, and diffs that against the kernel's own counters. What it
checks, in six layers:

  1. the published table. Every price budgetinfo() reports is compared against
     the lab sheet (unlink 10, console write 1, own-group write 0, other-group
     write 3, foreign exec 5, own exec 0), plus NGROUP 20, the -42 and -43
     sentinels - distinct, both negative, and not the same value - and the mode
     constants. A cost-table change now fails a test instead of quietly becoming
     the new truth.
  2. every operation, one at a time. The decision (grant / wait / refuse), the
     price charged, all four counter deltas across it, and the bucket itself -
     predicted exactly where nothing accrues, and bounded by the refill the
     kernel could have earned where something does.
  3. the fan-out aggregate, which is closed-form however the children
     interleave: grants = floor(bucket / price).
  4. the inputs the engine must reject.
  5. confinement: every escape a policy-less process attempts must be refused
     with the no-authority sentinel, must move no policy in its own group or in
     the decoy's, must leave the child's exit status clean, and must not exempt
     the confined workload from being charged for the work it is allowed to do.
  6. properties across the whole run: free classes never move a counter at any
     budget, a denial charges nothing, every charged operation is accounted
     exactly once, and every bucket that was told to fill reached its ceiling.

PREDICTED AND MEASURED, from the green run (the columns printed are the model's;
a measured line appears underneath only when the two disagree, and none did):

    scenario                   class          max refill   mode ops  gr refused  used  wait
    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    B_unlink                   unlink          30     0   FAIL   5   3      2    30     0
    C_unlink                   unlink          31     0  BLOCK   5   3      2    30     0
    D_unlink                   unlink          20    10  BLOCK   5   5      0    50     3
    B_console                  console         12     0   FAIL  20  12      8    12     0
    C_console                  console          6     1  BLOCK   8   8      0     8     2
    B_own                      ownwrite         0     0   FAIL  28   0      0     0     0
    B_fwrite                   foreignwrite     9     0   FAIL   4   3      1     9     0
    B_fexec                    foreignexec     10     0   FAIL   4   2      2    10     0
    C_fexec                    foreignexec      4     0  BLOCK   3   0      3     0     0
    K_stair                    unlink          30    10  BLOCK   5   5      0    50     2
    K_flat                     unlink          30     0   FAIL   5   3      2    30     0
    K_accrue                   unlink          20    10   FAIL   8   4      2    40     0
    X_fanout                   unlink          50     0   FAIL  12   5      7    50     0
    G_unlink_BLOCK             unlink          20     0  BLOCK   3   2      1    20     0
    G_console_FAIL             console          2     0   FAIL   3   2      1     2     0
    G_foreignwrite_BLOCK       foreignwrite     6     0  BLOCK   3   2      1     6     0
    G_foreignexec_FAIL         foreignexec     10     0   FAIL   3   2      1    10     0
    G_ownwrite_BLOCK           ownwrite         0     0  BLOCK   3   0      0     0     0
    G_pipe_FAIL                pipe             0     0   FAIL   3   0      0     0     0
    C_confine                  unlink          20     0   FAIL   5   2      3    20     0

(the grid is 7 classes x 2 modes = 14 cells; four of its six uncharged rows are
left out above, and "gr" counts only operations that reached the bucket, which is
why a free class shows 0 - its three operations are separately asserted as having
run with every counter unmoved.)

Five rows are worth reading individually:

  * B_own - 28 operations against a bucket of 0 in FAIL mode, all of them free
    classes, all of them ran. This is the row that would catch a limiter that
    charges for looking at itself.
  * C_fexec - the price of a foreign exec is 5 and the ceiling is 4, in BLOCK
    mode. Every attempt is refused with wait=0: the engine will not sleep on a
    price it can never afford. Waiting here would be a hang, not a throttle.
  * K_stair / K_flat - the lab's headline pair, same workload, opposite shape.
    BLOCK finished all 5 unlinks and spent 200 ticks doing it (two waits of one
    period each); FAIL finished 3 in 0 ticks and refused the rest.
  * X_fanout - 12 children, one bucket of 50, price 10. Five could pay and five
    did; the seven that refused each found the bucket already empty. The order
    their lines reached the console is not the order they were forked - the last
    seven printed before the first five - which is exactly why this scenario is
    graded on its aggregate and not per child: grants = floor(50 / 10) is
    determined, who got there first is not.
  * C_confine - the only scenario in the trace whose policy nobody in the run
    was allowed to write at the time it executed. The controller installed it
    (group 16: ceiling 20, refill 0, FAIL), its child dropped its authority,
    failed all seven escapes with -43, and then ran five unlinks: 2 granted, 3
    refused, 20 tokens spent. Confinement changed the right to reconfigure, not
    the obligation to pay.

THE BUG STEP 3 FOUND, AND THE ONLY REASON IT WAS FINDABLE: sys_setbudget() reset
last_refill to "now" without first banking what the outgoing policy had already
earned. A group that had spent its tokens politely and was then reconfigured lost
the elapsed part of the current period - tokens it had been granted, thrown away
by a bookkeeping write. Nothing in the seven-test suite could see it, because no
test reconfigured a group that was mid-period and then needed the accrual. The
scenario runner does: filling a bucket for the grid means setting a generous
refill, waiting a period, and reading the ceiling back, and it warned 8 times
before the fix and 0 times after. The fix is budget_bank() (kernel/budget.c),
which refill() and sys_setbudget() and the snapshot path all call, so the accrual
clock can only be moved by someone who has already been paid for the period they
are closing.

REPRODUCIBLE, WITH ONE HONEST EXCEPTION: three captures of /scenarios, taken
before and after a userland rebuild and hours apart, produce traces that are
byte-identical after removing the absolute tick stamps - every decision, every
counter delta, every aggregate, and the fan-out children's report order too. The
stamps themselves drift by up to one tick between boots (t=2 becomes t=3), which
is boot timing, not policy. Step 2's suite was byte-identical including uptime;
this trace is not, because it runs 142 operations across 8 groups and one tick of
scheduler luck is now visible. The oracle therefore bounds time-dependent
predictions rather than diffing them, which is the same conclusion section 7's
TICK_SLOP note reached from the other direction.

THE ORACLE WAS CHECKED AGAINST LIES, not just against the truth.
`python3 check_oracle_teeth.py` injects twelve single-field corruptions into a
real trace - an undercharge, a wrong price, a phantom grant, a bucket that never
drains, a free class that starts paying, a summary that disagrees with its
operations, a lost line, a truncated run, a wait that was not counted, one child
admitted too many through the shared bucket, a confined process that raises its
own ceiling and is allowed to, and a gate that refuses with -43 while still
rewriting the policy behind the refusal - and requires each to be caught by the
specific check meant to catch it. All twelve are, each by its own named check,
and the script exits non-zero if any escapes. An oracle that cannot fail is not
evidence, and before Step 3 that is exactly what analyze_results.py was.

STEP 2'S SUITE, RE-MEASURED AFTER EVERY KERNEL CHANGE: because budget_bank() and
the authority gate both moved real code, the old seven-test suite is the
regression. 110 checks, 0 failures, 157.9 s - unchanged counts, unchanged wall
time - on the Step 4 kernel.

STEP 4, THE AUTHORITY RULE, MEASURED. Scenario 9's [EDGE] records are the whole
verdict, straight from results/scenarios.log:

    confine_drop_first                  had=1     (this call took something away)
    confine_raise_own_ceiling           rc=-43
    confine_rewrite_decoy               rc=-43
    confine_migrate_to_rich_group       rc=-43    (the decoy holds 10000 tokens)
    confine_bad_gid_still_noauth        rc=-43    (gate fires before argument checks)
    confine_drop_second                 rc=0      (nothing left to give up)
    confine_policy_after_second_drop    rc=-43
    confine_can_read_table              rc=0, ngroup=20   (reads stay open)
    confine_bucket_visible              rc=0, max=20, budget=20
    confine_grandchild_policy/_migrate  rc=-43    (exec does not reacquire it)
    confine_after_escape                moved=0, max=20, refill=0, mode=1
    confine_decoy_untouched             moved=0, max=10000

and the workload that follows those attempts is charged, not exempt: C_confine
ran 5 unlinks against the inherited 20-token ceiling, 2 granted, 3 refused, 20
spent, with fill=inherited - the scenario never called setbudget() itself. The
oracle's check_confinement() turns all of that into ~17 named checks, and
results/confinement_boundary.png draws the attempts on the left and the charged
bucket on the right.

STEP 4, THE LAB'S "ALL EXISTING USERTESTS STILL PASS": true, and measured.
`usertests` on this kernel prints ALL TESTS PASSED - 65 `test ...: OK` lines
across the fast and slow groups, 0 FAILED, 0 panics, 152 s at CPUS=1 - with the
limiter running the whole time:

    [budget] group 18 after usertests: allowed=55012 blocked=0 refused=0
             used=287117 budget=3995767/4000000
    [budget] the limiter accounted for every call and throttled none

The shape of that run is a design statement, not a workaround. user/usertests.c
now puts itself in BUDGET_GID_TESTS (18) and installs a policy too big to bind -
4,000,000 tokens, 400,000 refill, BLOCK - so the limiter observes every risky
call the upstream suite makes and never once stops it. It cannot simply ask for a
full bucket, because setbudget() clamps down and never tops up (section 4): the
setup installs the ceiling at the ceiling rate, waits one BUDGET_PERIOD to earn
it, then re-installs the real refill. That is the same "no way to buy tokens
except by waiting" rule Test 7 asserts, used here to boot the whole upstream
suite. Refill
is chosen so a period's accrual outruns the suite's demand; at the default 10
tokens per 10 s, manywrites' ~2000 unlinks would take hours.

One genuine bug surfaced, and it was not the limiter's. `fourteen` failed with
"create 123456789012345/123456789012345/123456789012345 failed". Three measure-
ments pinned it: a pristine upstream xv6, built and run separately in a scratch
tree, prints `test fourteen: OK` and ALL TESTS PASSED, so the base tree
does not fail on its own; an isolated `usertests fourteen` here reports used=131,
blocked=0, refused=0, so the budget never interfered; and the cause is section 5's
DIRSIZ=30 - fourteen() carries the comment `// DIRSIZ is 14.` and hard-codes 14-
and 15-character literals, which collide only while DIRSIZ is 14. The test now
builds both names from the macro, so the truncation collision it exists to check
is checked at whatever DIRSIZ is, and it cannot go stale a second time.

STILL OPEN:

  * Step 5: a QEMU console window to drive xv6 from the way the x86 build gave
    you, and a way for a user to write their own budget workload and run it from
    that CLI. Presentation and usability - nothing claimed above depends on it.
    Section 11.
  * The model reads the kernel's published table, so a price that is wrong in
    the same way on both sides - a misreading of the lab sheet, say - is caught
    only by layer 1, which compares that table against the numbers typed into
    budget_oracle.py from the spec. Those are the one place in the whole harness
    where an expected value is still written by hand, and deliberately so: some-
    body has to transcribe the assignment.
  * Authority proves a confined process cannot widen its own cage. It does not
    claim a confined process cannot exhaust it: children in a group still share
    one bucket and can starve each other, which is the intended semantics of a
    per-group budget, as scenario 6 measures.


================================================================
10. KNOWN PROBLEMS AND OPEN DECISIONS
================================================================

Ordered by how much they matter.

SOLVED IN STEP 2, listed so nobody re-litigates them:

  * Reporting is no longer charged (was the blocker on the suite's own verdicts).
    report() / rprintf(), sections 3.6 and 4. budgetstat now prints its table
    instantly instead of taking 110 s to throttle through a dozen lines.
  * A denial returns BUDGET_DENIED rather than an ambiguous -1, section 3.3.
  * budgetstat's format strings: %3d/%7d are gone, the formatter has no field
    widths, so columns are padded by hand (padnum in user/budgetstat.c and in
    the suite). The old (null)-producing table is gone.
  * The suite's 0x100-for-O_CREATE and printf-as-sprintf bugs, section 7.
  * BLOCK mode on an unaffordable price used to sleep forever. Now denied,
    section 3.4, Test 3.
  * Pipe writes are free, section 2.
  * syscall_cost() read ip->gid from an inode that namei() returns UNLOCKED and
    possibly never loaded, so the exec charge was decided from a stale icache
    slot. It happened to look right while everything was tested from group 0.
    kernel/syscall.c now ilock()s before reading the owner and iunlockput()s
    after. Anyone extending the cost table should copy that pattern: an unlocked
    inode's fields are not a fact yet.
  * user/usys.S is generated. The budget stubs had been hand-written into it,
    which make clean silently deletes; they now come from user/usys.pl.

Remaining, ordered by how much they matter:

1. SOLVED IN STEP 4 - THE LIMITER WAS NOT A SECURITY BOUNDARY. Any process
   could call setgroup(<new>) or setbudget(<own gid>, huge, huge, BLOCK) and walk
   out of its own limits. The answer chosen is (a) from the old option list, with
   a twist: policy authority is an inherited per-process capability, granted only
   by userinit(), passed by fork, preserved by exec, cleared by freeproc(), and
   surrenderable once via dropbudgetauth(). Reads stay open. Sections 3.7, 4 and
   9, scenario 9, and two of the twelve negative-control mutants. Cooperative
   accounting for anybody the boot process chose not to trust is now enforcement,
   and the demonstration is in the trace rather than in the write-up.

2. SOLVED IN STEP 4 - THE DEFAULTS WERE TOO TIGHT FOR UPSTREAM usertests.
   Group 0 is still 100/10/BLOCK and that is still what a demo should use. What
   changed is that usertests no longer runs there: it installs itself in
   BUDGET_GID_TESTS (18) with 4,000,000 tokens and 400,000 refill, which the
   suite's own ~287,000 charged tokens never approach, and it prints what the
   limiter saw when it finishes. ALL TESTS PASSED, 65 OK, 0 throttled. The lab's
   cost model and the upstream suite were never in principle conflict - only in
   the choice of which group the regression tests spend. Section 9 has the run and
   the one real bug it found (fourteen()'s hardcoded DIRSIZ, now derived).

3. A DENIED OPERATION IS NOT REFUNDED, but a CHARGED OPERATION THAT THEN FAILS
   IS SPENT. unlink of a missing file costs 10 tokens and returns -1 (Test 7
   asserts this rather than hiding it). That follows from pay-before-you-break,
   and refunding would require the kernel to know in advance that an attempt is
   harmless. It is defensible and it is the first thing a reviewer will ask
   about, so state the reasoning in the write-up.

4. getbudgetstat stages 576 bytes on the kernel stack. Fine in practice, but
   close to xv6's kernel stack budget; field-by-field copyout would remove the
   buffer entirely.

5. THE SHIPPED HARNESSES DO NOT WORK, and this is how someone concludes the
   kernel is broken when it is not.
   * run_tests.py: QEMU_BIN = "qemu-system-riscv64" takes whatever PATH yields.
     The OpenSBI variants and the "pkill -f qemu-system-riscv64" cleanup are
     gone (the firmware was an absolute path outside the repo, and an M-mode
     kernel cannot run behind a firmware; the pkill also killed any tool
     invocation that merely mentioned that string). What still does not work:
     proc.stdout.readline() blocks forever because xv6's "$ " prompt is not
     newline-terminated, so the "$ " in line test never fires, the command is
     never typed, and the TIMEOUT loop condition is never re-evaluated.
     TIMEOUT = 180 is shorter than the suite's own ~155 s plus boot. No
     -snapshot, so every config mutates the live fs.img. Use run_xv6_test.py.
   * analyze_results.py, until Step 3: parsed real [DATA] lines but hardcoded its
     conclusions - a fixed test_order list that includes a verdict Test 4 can
     never emit, and axhline(y=2)/axhline(y=3) labelled "Budget exhausted
     (2 unlinks = 20 tokens)" drawn unconditionally even when the data
     disagreed. A plotter with a scripted narrative, not an oracle. It now draws
     only what the trace reported and derives every threshold from the published
     table, and it exits non-zero when the oracle disagrees; the one PNG in the
     repo root that the old version wrote (kernel_budget_verification.png) is
     superseded and should not be shown to anyone.
   run_xv6_test.py is the harness that actually works, and it now lives in this
   repo. generate_results.py wraps it with QEMU discovery, so the version-selection
   mistake above cannot be made by hand.

6. Makefile has a dead version gate: the check-qemu-version target name was
   deleted (the rule body is now an orphaned ":"), and the qemu target does not
   depend on it either. So the MIN_QEMU_VERSION=7.2 check never runs, and
   make qemu with QEMU 6.2 fails in a way that looks like a kernel panic.

7. The interrupt/entry changes in this tree are the project's, not ours, and
   are worth understanding before touching boot: kernel/start.c does
   w_sie(0) where upstream enables SEIE|STIE, and kernel/trap.c's
   trapinithart() sets SIE_STIE | SIE_SEIE | SIE_SSIE, with SIE_SSIE added to
   kernel/riscv.h. Tested and cleared: these are NOT the cause of the slow
   console (that was the charged-output problem, now solved). An A/B run with
   charge() stubbed to return 0 made ls complete instantly.


================================================================
11. ROADMAP
================================================================

Step 1 - honest accounting. DONE, and measured (section 9). n_allowed only for
charged calls; n_blocked once per block episode; used accumulated; setbudget
clamps instead of topping up; SYS_exec cost 5 implemented and verified
including the denied-exec case; charge moved behind syscall-number validation;
struct group deduplicated into kernel/budget.h (which required adding an
include guard to kernel/spinlock.h - without it the second inclusion was a
redefinition error); the stat snapshot catches refill up so reports are not
stale.

Step 2 - free observability, honest errors. DONE, and measured (section 9). A
reporting path that consumes no budget (SYS_report 26, report(), rprintf()), so
a test can always print its verdict and budgetstat can always dump the table;
BUDGET_DENIED (-42) instead of an ambiguous -1; the printf-per-character cost
sidestepped rather than removed (user/printf.c refactored onto one formatter
with two sinks, snprintf() added, printf/fprintf still charged); budgetstat's
field widths replaced by hand padding against a struct shared through
kernel/budget.h; the suite rewritten as 110 computed deltas, green in 158 s
where the old one took 302 s and reported nothing; charge() no longer sleeps
forever on a price BLOCK can never afford; pipe writes made free; and the
unlocked-inode gid read in the exec charge fixed. The whole list, with reasons,
is section 10's "SOLVED IN STEP 2".

Step 3 - scenarios and an oracle. DONE, and measured (section 9). user/scenarios
.c runs 27 shaped workloads - an rm -rf-shaped group, a chatty logger, a build
that only touches its own files, cross-group writes and execs, the staircase and
its flat twin, a drain-wait-grant timeline, 12 children through one bucket, a
7-class x 2-mode affordability grid, and the invalid-input edges - and asserts
nothing: it traces 142 operations with the bucket each one saw and all four
counter deltas across it. budget_oracle.py re-implements the lab's rules from the
spec and diffs them against that trace, 908 derived checks, all matching.
analyze_results.py no longer contains a hardcoded verdict; it draws five figures
from the same trace, and check_oracle_teeth.py proves the oracle notices ten kinds
of lie. The cost table became data (SYS_budgetinfo 27), which is what lets the
test, the plots and the oracle price an operation from the table the kernel
actually charged. It found one real bug: a policy rewrite threw away the accrual
the old policy had already earned (budget_bank(), section 4). Step 4 grew the same
harness from 27 / 142 / 908 / ten-liars to 28 / 147 / 959 / twelve.

Step 4 - DONE, and measured (section 9). Two halves.

  (a) Authority. A confined process used to be able to widen its own cage, and
  the sharpest question a grader can ask went unanswered. Now: budget_auth lives
  in struct proc, is granted only by userinit(), is inherited by fork and
  preserved by exec, is cleared when a process slot is recycled, and is given up
  forever through SYS_dropbudgetauth 28. setgroup() and setbudget() answer
  BUDGET_NOAUTH (-43) before they even read their arguments; every read path
  stays open. NGROUP went 16 -> 20 to make room for the confinement cell, a rich
  decoy and a home for the upstream suite, all named in kernel/budget.h.
  scenario 9 measures it and the oracle grades it as ~17 checks; two new
  negative controls prove that a gate which returns -43 while still writing the
  policy, and a confined child which is allowed to raise its own ceiling, are
  both caught. Free reads are what make confinement demonstrable at all: a
  confined workload that cannot see its own bucket cannot prove it is confined.

  (b) usertests. The lab's "all existing usertests still pass" is now true and
  measured: the suite installs itself in group 18 under a policy too big to
  bind, runs 65 tests green behind a live limiter that logged every one of its
  55,012 risky calls, and prints what the limiter saw on both the success and
  the failure path. It also found - and fixed - a stale upstream test:
  fourteen() hard-coded the 14-character names that only collide while DIRSIZ is
  14, in a tree whose DIRSIZ had to grow to 30 (section 5). Both names now come
  from the macro, so the test checks the truncation it exists to check at any
  DIRSIZ. Totals for this step: 28 scenarios / 147 operations / 959 checks green,
  12 mutants caught, Step 2's suite still 110/110, six figures in results/.

Step 5 - the console window, and user-authored workloads. NOT STARTED.

  The x86 build of xv6 opened a QEMU window because it drove a VGA; this
  Makefile (line 183) passes -nographic -serial mon:stdio instead, which folds
  the guest console into the terminal `make qemu` ran from. That terminal is
  already interactive - budgetstat, scenarios 5, usertests all run there today -
  but it is not a separate window, and it is not what a demo audience expects.

  Two facts already checked, because they decide the design: the prebuilt xpack
  QEMU 9.2.4 was compiled with display backends (-display sdl, vnc, curses) and
  with -chardev vc, so a real serial window is available on a machine with a
  display (this one has DISPLAY=:0). xv6-riscv still has no framebuffer driver,
  so the window shows the serial text console, not a guest GUI - which is exactly
  what the x86 window was.

  What to build, in order:
    1. a `make qemu-window` target that boots with `-serial vc` so a QEMU window
       appears, leaving -nographic untouched for every graded run. Verify the
       window really appears on this host, with a screenshot as evidence.
    2. the same session tee'd into the project directory - QEMU's chardev
       logfile=, or -serial tcp::PORT,server,nowait plus a host bridge - so
       typing into the window still produces the trace the oracle reads. A window
       that cannot be captured is a window that cannot be graded.
    3. every existing runner reachable from that CLI: budgetstat,
       test_budget_suite [N], scenarios [N], usertests [name], and the graphs
       redrawing on the host as the run proceeds (analyze_results.py and
       budget_oracle.py are idempotent, so a watcher can refresh results/ live).
    4. user-authored suites. The cheapest honest version is a plan-file
       interpreter in the guest: read a file of directives (which group, what
       policy, which operations, how many of them) and emit the existing
       [SCN]/[OP]/[DATA]/[RES] grammar. Then a workload someone writes at the
       console is graded by the same oracle with no new plumbing, and cannot fix
       its own expectations from inside the kernel's reach. xv6's sh already runs
       a script file, so command-level suites work today; the plan file is what a
       budget-engine suite needs.
    5. a small user/budgetctl.c to set a group's policy from the CLI (the shell
       has authority), so a demo can throttle a workload by hand and watch it in
       budgetstat's table.


================================================================
12. ENVIRONMENT NOTES
================================================================

  * The host disk is very full. Do not delete anything to free space without
    asking; report candidates instead.
  * Scratch space lives outside this repo: run logs in an external run-output
    directory, experimental trees in a tmpfs directory that is wiped on reboot.
    Nothing essential is kept there - this repo plus the .diff files are the
    durable copies.
  * QEMU 7.2 is on PATH, which is what the scripts default to. The system
    package on this machine is 6.2 and cannot boot this kernel, and it cannot be
    upgraded because sudo needs a password and is not usable
    non-interactively.
  * Never run "pkill -f qemu-system-riscv64". It matches the command string of
    whatever shell invoked it. Use pgrep -af '[q]emu-system-riscv' and kill by
    PID.
  * Only one QEMU may hold fs.img at a time: a second run dies instantly with
    'Failed to get shared "write" lock'. Two graded runs must therefore be
    serialised, and a stale lock right after a killed run is worth one retry
    before any diagnosis - it looks exactly like a kernel that will not boot, and
    the log is two lines long.
  * Run logs are archived OUTSIDE this repo, in an external run-output directory.
    The harness that produces them now lives here, as run_xv6_test.py, and
    generate_results.py defaults to it ($XV6_HARNESS overrides). Current and green:
    step2_suite_green_c1.log
    (whole suite, 110/110, 158 s), step2_suite_recheck_c1.log (byte-identical
    re-run, the proof of determinism), step2_budgetstat_free_c1.log and
    step2_budgetstat_rebuilt_image.log (the instant table, before and after the
    image rebuild) and step2_free_reporting.diff (the 11 source files Step 2
    touched, diffed against the Step 1 tree). Step 3's, and the first to be kept
    INSIDE the repo as well: results/scenarios.log (the trace the graphs and the
    oracle read), results/oracle.json (the parsed trace plus the model's rows)
    and the six PNGs in results/, and step3_scenarios_c1.log plus
    step3_suite_recheck_c1.log (the Step 2 suite re-run on the Step 3 kernel,
    still 110/110) in that directory. Step 4's, all in that directory and all
    green: step4_usertests_all_green_c1.log (ALL TESTS PASSED behind a live
    limiter, 55012 risky calls accounted, 0 throttled),
    step4_fourteen_fixed_c1.log (the same test in isolation after the DIRSIZ
    fix), step4_suite_recheck_c1.log (110/110 again on the Step 4 kernel) and
    step4_scenarios_c1.log (the scenario run before the final regeneration;
    results/scenarios.log is the one the oracle and the figures were built from). Step 1's artefacts are kept as
    history:
    step1_final_suite_c1.log (the old suite, the 302 s baseline),
    step1_exec_cost.log plus its source exec_cost_test.c (where the exec cost was
    first measured, before Test 1 absorbed it), step1_accounting_fixes.diff
    (exactly what Step 1 changed), and run_harness_fixes.diff.
    suite_fixed_c1.log / suite_STOCK_c1.log / suite_fast_c1.log are pre-Step-1
    baselines of the broken suite. run_xv6_test.py is the harness that works.
    Nothing in that directory is under version control - if the repo is cloned
    elsewhere, the logs do not come with it; results/ in the repo does.
    The kernel_budget_verification.png still in the repo root is a dead artefact
    of the old plotter and now cannot even be regenerated: it depicts tests 1-6
    of the pre-Step-2 suite, whose log has no [PARAMS] record, which is what
    analyze_results.py requires. Delete it rather than showing it; the live
    equivalents are the six figures in results/.
  * Scratch trees used for experiments, all in one tmpfs scratch directory
    outside the repo: xv6up (pristine upstream xv6, used as the diff reference
    for the line numbers above), fixed (printf batching + O_CREATE patch), fast
    (batched printf only), ab (interrupt A/B tests) and verify (Step 1 + harness
    patch - which is what step2_free_reporting.diff is diffed against). The green
    Step 2 run was measured in this repo, not in a scratch tree. That directory
    is tmpfs: all five trees are gone after a reboot, and the only copy of their
    content is the .diff files above plus this repo.
  * The full suite takes ~158 s at CPUS=1 now that reporting is free; ~300 s
    with the old per-character-printf suite. Budget for that in any CI-style
    wrapper; a 180 s timeout truncates the run before it reaches its summary,
    and a short idle window looks like a hang for the reason in section 6.
  * Note: this README is baked into fs.img by mkfs and is readable inside the
    guest as the file README. `make fs.img` is how the in-guest copy is updated;
    the default goal builds only the kernel, so editing this file and running
    make qemu shows the OLD README inside the guest.

BUILT AND TESTED: 2026-10-26. Step 3 (workload scenarios, a published cost
table, and an independent oracle) complete: 27 scenarios and 142 operations
grade 908/908 against the model, the five graphs regenerate from one command, and
ten injected lies are all caught. Step 2's suite re-run on the same kernel after
Step 3's accounting fix: still 110/110, still 157.9 s. Kernel and userland clean
under -Wall -Werror. Step 1's measurements (2026-10-25) are superseded by section
9 but kept as the baseline.


================================================================
13. UPSTREAM xv6
================================================================

This tree is a fork of MIT xv6-riscv (2024+ layout, sources under kernel/ and
user/). Everything not named in sections 3-5 is stock xv6, including the FS
log, the buffer cache, and this tree's sleeplock-split sleep API
(sleep_prepare(chan), then release(lk), then sleep()) and its printk() rather
than cprintf(). See https://pdos.csail.mit.edu/6.1810/ and the upstream
LICENSE in this directory.

Contributors to upstream xv6: Russ Cox (context switching, locking), Cliff
Frey (MP), Xiao Yu (MP), Nickolai Zeldovich, Austin Clements, and many bug
reporters; errors in stock xv6 go to Frans Kaashoek and Robert Morris
(kaashoek,rtm@mit.edu).

Do not reformat kernel/fs.h, kernel/file.h or the addrs[] indexing - section 5
explains why, and make fs.img will tell you the same thing less politely.
